[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Refactoring Legacy Code: Identifying Opportunities for Design Patterns"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Refactoring legacy codebases can be intimidating. Often characterized by massive methods, tight coupling, and excessive conditional logic, legacy code resists change and breeds bugs. However, legacy code is also a rich landscape for applying proven design patterns. This tutorial explores how to identify architectural pain points in existing code and refactor them into clean, maintainable structures using standard design patterns."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Phase 1: Recognizing Code Smells"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before applying any design pattern, you must identify the symptoms of poor design, commonly known as code smells. Design patterns are solutions to specific architectural problems, so matching the smell to the pattern is the crucial first step."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Long Switch/If-Else Statements: Often indicates an opportunity for the Strategy Pattern or Polymorphism."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"God Objects: Classes that know too much or do too much, pointing to the Single Responsibility Principle and the Facade or Mediator patterns."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Widespread Object Creation: Direct instantiation using the 'new' keyword everywhere leads to tight coupling, which is best solved with Factory or Dependency Injection patterns."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Phase 2: Case Study - The Conditional Nightmare"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a legacy notification service responsible for sending messages via Email, SMS, or Push notification. The original code relies heavily on type checking and conditional branching."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class NotificationManager {
    public void sendNotification(String type, String message) {
        if (type.equals("EMAIL")) {
            // Complex email logic
            System.out.println("Sending Email: " + message);
        } else if (type.equals("SMS")) {
            // Complex SMS logic
            System.out.println("Sending SMS: " + message);
        } else if (type.equals("PUSH")) {
            // Complex Push logic
            System.out.println("Sending Push: " + message);
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown type");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every time a new notification channel is added, developers must modify this class, violating the Open-Closed Principle. This is a classic indicator for the Strategy Pattern."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Phase 3: Applying the Strategy Pattern"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To refactor this, we extract the behaviors into separate classes implementing a common interface. First, define the interface:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public interface NotificationStrategy {
    void send(String message);
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, implement concrete classes for each notification type:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class EmailNotification implements NotificationStrategy {
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending Email: " + message);
    }
}

public class SmsNotification implements NotificationStrategy {
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending SMS: " + message);
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Phase 4: Simplifying the Client Code"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Finally, refactor the manager class to utilize a map or registry to resolve strategies dynamically, completely removing the conditional statements."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Map;

public class RefactoredNotificationManager {
    private final Map<String, NotificationStrategy> strategies;

    public RefactoredNotificationManager(Map<String, NotificationStrategy> strategies) {
        this.strategies = strategies;
    }

    public void sendNotification(String type, String message) {
        NotificationStrategy strategy = strategies.get(type);
        if (strategy == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown type");
        }
        strategy.send(message);
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"By identifying code smells like complex conditionals, you can systematically refactor legacy applications into modular, pattern-driven architectures. Always ensure you have a robust suite of automated tests before initiating refactoring to guarantee behavior remains unchanged."}]
